To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove the following:\n\n![](images/83/833e64bb77220a52ec87ac4c333439ddf6097151aa992508070af2614a51ae36.svg)\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n![](images/0f/0fd03d4f705ce8e2eb6969f528c847d274acd7ccaf816e2510f331ed8544314c.svg)\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k \\geq 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}k \\geq 1},$ we have\n![](images/79/79ca61162feea82659e68725144768b96b947d21c7d0273f0435b07875b3b400.svg)\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n![](images/11/11c05d918501f3e52784129b1125fc456d6f61b40f36124ca8666d206fee1c60.svg)\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we're shown that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ![](images/df/dff83649ba6f41c29c1578c82159beff07d1355f4ad730dc4485e5af25de8a93.svg)\nfor all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$" 13194139533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186082926 1074 "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1)$. To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n> **Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1$.\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some\n\n$$\nn=k \\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n)$.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n> **Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}k \\geq 1}$, we have\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1)$. We start by writing down $P(k+1)$.\n\n> **Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we're shown that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1)$, the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n> **Conclusion**: Therefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n> for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$." 13194139533581 17592186045417 false] [17592186082927 1071 "Use mathematical induction to prove that\n![](images/07/07547d9d90fe3939d5ce9a528a2fd37a56980b566d17ee057436b6fcc4694fff.svg)\nfor all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$" 13194139533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186082927 1071 "Use mathematical induction to prove that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$." 13194139533581 17592186045417 false] [17592186082927 1074 "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3), \\ldots$, where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n- $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1)$. To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\n> We proceed using induction on $n$.\n> **Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1$.\n> For the left-hand side, we have\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}^{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$,\n> and for the right-hand side, we have\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4^{1} - 1 & 4^{1}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\n> Thus, our statement is true for $n=1$.\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some\n\n$$\nn=k\\geq 1\n$$\n\n> **Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1$, we have\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$. Let's start by writing down $P(k+1)$. This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n> **Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\n> We write\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}^{k + 1}|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}^{k} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\n> By the inductive hypothesis, we have\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}^{k} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4^{k} - 1 & 4^{k}\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4^{k} - 1 + 3 \\cdot 4^{k} & 4 \\cdot 4^{k}\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 \\cdot 4^{k} - 1 & 4 \\cdot 4^{k}\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4^{k + 1} - 1 & 4^{k + 1}\\end{bmatrix}$,\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, by the principle of induction,\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n> is true for all $n\\geq 1$." 13194139533581 17592186045417 false] [17592186082927 1074 "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3), \\ldots,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n![](images/d7/d7e3e5ed2dc60dd8a7a40711798ba589649a7a85cfb01630bdc0e63d779eee8e.svg)\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ {4}^{1}-1 & {4}^{1}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\geq 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1,$ we have\n![](images/fc/fc4407495fc6621f5e80daf8846c11206289d4be5fe4a8af9f67721291216a89.svg)\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n![](images/7f/7fc2b8a2b2e0ec12f515afcb2d730adf2747ef3cea41f2c97d2d87df9e139118.svg)\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows:\n\n![](images/09/099b3ed6a4c4dd958d1df9dbf507c775cf9a4a9f42ed661e5f8937f70cd46109.svg)\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ {4}^{k}-1 & {4}^{k}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ {4}^{k}-1+3\\cdot {4}^{k} & 4\\cdot {4}^{k}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4\\cdot {4}^{k}-1 & 4\\cdot {4}^{k}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ {4}^{k+1}-1 & {4}^{k+1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n![](images/f6/f67905088dd4d08876f93a46f2997497279c9eaadcdb4cc501ca932cb46265c2.svg)\nis true for all $n\\geq 1.$" 13194139533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186083176 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409785 13194139533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186083176 1038 #edb/ref 17592186409786 13194139533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186083462 1050 480.0 13194139533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186084506 1064 "Recall that the **principle of induction** states the following:\n\n> *Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), \\, P(2), \\, P(3), \\, \\ldots$, where*\n> - *$P(1)$ is true, and*\n> - *$P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N$.*\n> *Then, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N$.*\n\nLet's recall our general strategy for proving results using induction:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how induction can be used to prove matrix identities." 13194139533581 17592186045417 false] [17592186084506 1064 "Recall that the **principle of induction** states the following: \n\n*Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), \\, P(2), \\, P(3), \\, \\ldots,$ where*\n\n- *$P(1)$ is true, and*\n\n- *$P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N.$*\n\n*Then, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$*\n\nLet's recall our general strategy for proving results using induction:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how induction can be used to prove matrix identities." 13194139533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186084520 1064 "Let's prove the following result using mathematical induction:\n\n![](images/f9/f9106bf23db3c56425757ea9b8b3aef7adf5cbe926212746c85c63e4193f308d.svg) for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n![](images/82/82fb358f5e84e37e035da6cfa101c65f6e43b9bad75c63299a56999b125c7f63.svg)\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2(1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\geq 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1,$ we have\n![](images/7e/7e1416be5b4270fdfe3051c74f1db5106556ed860c9f88fcbdd866f48ff3339b.svg)\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n![](images/e0/e0042401a1070f9f1977a7005f6875f8bc14886842a908fcc78f33678fdcb39b.svg)\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows: \n\n![](images/06/06dc062c2c15fba27384ac9ec89ddbe2827ea1901ed74ccef1f97dcf11991d54.svg)\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2+2k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2(k+1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n![](images/2a/2a3c4d7370e4036aea2b0ad07bdee4a244ea828bef025178c8919be08a410b23.svg)\nis true for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$" 13194139533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186084520 1064 "Let's prove the following result using mathematical induction:\n\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1)$. To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\n> **Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1$.\n> For the left-hand side, we have\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$,\n> and for the right-hand side, we have\n> $\\begin{bmat