533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186409785 1072 #uuid "6a379f14-e14d-5731-8273-6d8b7d1a1228" 13194139533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186409785 1074 "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove the following:\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{n} & \\frac{{3}^{n}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{1} & \\frac{{3}^{1}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}=[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{k} & \\frac{{3}^{k}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDue to the base case, we already know that the statement is true for some value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1}=[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{k+1} & \\frac{{3}^{k+1}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we're shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{n} & \\frac{{3}^{n}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nfor all $n\\in N.$" 13194139533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186409786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409771 13194139533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186409786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409773 13194139533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186409786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409775 13194139533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186409786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409776 13194139533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186409786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409778 13194139533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186409786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409779 13194139533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186409786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409782 13194139533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186409786 1069 "q-183767" 13194139533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186409786 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186409786 1071 "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5n & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{for all }\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements." 13194139533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186409786 1072 #uuid "c2d53f1a-2ddc-5a46-80ab-ba5c178f639a" 13194139533581 17592186045417 true] [17592186409786 1074 "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove the following:\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5n & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5(1) & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5\\,\\,k & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5(k+1) & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we're shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5n & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nfor all $n\\in N.$" 13194139533581 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-07cb646c-c85b-56c9-bdf0-ce1d53f9d356" 17592186409717 